.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt PS 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm ps
.Nd list this session's processes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a builtin of
.Xr absd-sh 1 ,
not a program: there is no
.Pa /bin/ps .
The shell asks the SSH server on the session's control channel, and the
server answers for the session that channel belongs to.
There is no way to name another session, and no list of every process in
the system exists.
.Pp
The answer has three parts:
.Bl -enum
.It
The session: its number (the SSH server's connection number), whether it
has a terminal, and how long ago it connected.
.It
The processes the server can name: those it holds a process handle to.
That is the session's shell alone.
The shell runs its commands in the foreground only, so while it runs
.Nm
it has none running, and no command's process appears.
.It
What the server can only count:
.Ql other holders: input N, file server channels M .
.Ar N
is how many more read ends of the session's input pipe exist than the
shell's own, and
.Ar M
how many more write ends of the session's file server channels than the
server's and the shell's.
Both are 0 in normal operation: commands cannot start programs, so the
shell and its commands are the whole session.
A nonzero count would be a process that belongs to this session in effect
but that no process of it can name or end (adopted by init); it keeps the
session's input and filesystem channel until it ends.
.El
.Pp
.Nm
may start a pipeline
.Pq Ql ps | wc -l
and may be redirected.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when the server answered.
1 when the shell has no session control channel (it was not started by the
SSH server for a session, for instance a shell started by another shell)
or the server did not answer.
2 when
.Nm
is given arguments.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
absd$ ps
session 3 (this session), terminal, connected 41 s
  PID PROGRAM
   23 /bin/absd-sh
other holders: input 0, file server channels 0
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr absd-sh 1 ,
.Xr process 7
.Sh CAVEATS
The counts are of handles, not processes: one leftover process that holds
both the input and a channel adds one to each.
They are a snapshot; a leftover process may end at any moment.
